[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is in the Campus 1 distribution switch/router mswitch0’s routing subsystem (FRRouting/OSPF). Campus PCs can reach each other and their local gateways (SVIs on mswitch0), but any off‑campus traffic is rejected at the gateway with ICMP net-unreachable. From pc5 to 10.5.0.1 we see “Destination Net Unreachable” sourced by 172.16.10.254, confirming the gateway itself has no route outward.

On mswitch0, the routing table only contains connected VLANs and the 10.2.2.0/24 link; there is no default route or learned routes (ip route shows only 172.16.10/20/30 and 10.2.2.0). Critically, frr.service is failed, and key FRR configs are empty: /etc/frr/ospfd.conf and zebra.conf are 0 bytes. With FRR down/unconfigured, mswitch0 no longer forms OSPF or installs a default route toward router16 (10.2.2.1). As a result, it returns net-unreachable to Campus 2/backbone destinations. Upstream, router11 no longer shows Campus 1 prefixes (no 172.16.10/20/30 entries in its OSPF RIB), which matches the on-call observation but is a symptom, not the cause.

Localization: mswitch0 (bond0/br0 toward router16, VLAN SVIs). Root cause: FRR on mswitch0 failed with missing/empty configuration, removing OSPF peering and external routes. Corrective fix: Restore mswitch0 routing—recreate proper /etc/frr/zebra.conf and /etc/frr/ospfd.conf (advertise 172.16.10/20/30 and run OSPF on 10.2.2.2 toward 10.2.2.1), then start frr. As an immediate workaround, add a static default route (ip route add default via 10.2.2.1) on mswitch0 to restore connectivity pending FRR repair. Restarting router11 is not required. 
=== END ===